=== PARITY (vs web Base64Tool.tsx) ===
  Data sources -> the web source's ONLY hook is `useTranslation`; it is a pure, synchronous,
    local transform (`btoa`/`atob` in a `useMemo`) with NO data hook, NO network, NO cache. The
    native parity therefore binds through a local P1/S8 state holder (Base64ToolModel) that owns
    `input` + `mode` and DERIVES `result` on read (the @Observable analogue of the web `useMemo`).
    The view performs no HTTP — there is none to perform.
  Transform -> Base64Codec.transform is a faithful port: encode rejects code points above 0xFF
    (the `btoa` domain; web throws -> here `.invalid`), decode is strict base64 (`atob`; malformed
    -> `.invalid`), blank input -> `.empty` (web `if (!inputVal) return ''`). Decode renders UTF-8
    when valid else a Latin-1 binary string (the `atob` return contract). Verified by executed XCTest
    (round-trip, non-Latin-1 reject, malformed reject, ASCII vectors `Hello World`<->`SGVsbG8gV29ybGQ=`).
  Composition -> ToolCard-style header (amber `curlybraces` glyph = web lucide `Braces` + color
    "amber" -> statusWarning token, title + description) · encode/decode segmented control (web two
    `Button`s, variant flips primary/ghost with the active mode, size sm -> TSButton .small) · input
    editor (web `Textarea` rows=3 + mode example overlay) · output panel (web recessed panel: "Output"
    label + CopyButton + monospaced cyan result -> mono font + accent token) · invalid + empty states.
  Shared components -> TSButton (Components/UI/Buttons) for the mode toggle; ContentUnavailableView
    for the empty state; everything else composed from the P1/S9 design tokens (Color.TS / Font.TS /
    TSSpacing / TSRadius). The reusable native `ToolCard` and `CopyButton` are separate surfaces
    (prompts 0010 / their own), so the card chrome + a UIKit/AppKit copy affordance are composed
    inline here rather than depended upon. Recharts -> none / Leaflet -> none (no chart/map).
  States (all render) -> the web source produces exactly THREE result states, reproduced here, each
    with a #if DEBUG preview and a unit test:
      • empty   (web hides the output panel) -> friendly ContentUnavailableView (contract: never a
                blank box), per encode/decode mode.
      • content (web `<pre>` output)         -> Base64OutputPanel (encoded OR decoded), copyable.
      • invalid (web `catch -> t('Invalid Input')`) -> inline danger treatment.
    The generic loading / stale / offline states from the prompt's state checklist have NO trigger
    on this surface: it is a synchronous local transform with no async data source (no spinner), no
    freshness window (nothing can be stale), and no network dependency (it works fully offline by
    nature). Reproducing them would fabricate behavior absent from the web spec; parity = matching
    the source, so they are intentionally omitted (Honesty rule 3, "no skip-and-assume": the source
    was read end-to-end; these branches genuinely do not exist).
  i18n -> all source keys resolve through the P1/S10 facade (Base64Strings, table "Base64Tool")
    with the web `t(key, default)` fallback: devtools.utils.base64 ("Base64"),
    devtools.utils.base64Desc ("Base64Desc", verbatim source default), Encode, Decode, Input Label,
    Output Label, Invalid Input -> ALL present in Base64Tool.strings, plus the native chrome/a11y
    keys (action.copy/copied, base64.emptyTitle/emptyHint, a11y.base64.emptyEncode/emptyDecode).
    No English literals in native code (every string from the facade; example vectors are data).
  a11y -> mode buttons carry .isSelected when active; the input editor an accessibilityLabel; the
    copy button a state-dependent label (Copy/Copied); the output panel is a contained element; the
    surface exposes Base64Accessibility.summary (mode + result/value, or the invalid message) as its
    combined VoiceOver label. Dynamic Type via tokens; the copy confirmation has no motion to respect.
  Telemetry -> Base64ToolModel.start() emits viewOpened(surface: "Base64Tool") exactly once via the
    P1/S11 seam (OSLog default; production injects the shared Telemetry.track(.screenView) adapter).
    Asserted by a spy test (fires once across two start() calls; slug == "Base64Tool").

